'use strict';

/**
 * BeHistorical Foundations Unit Examination, Version 3.
 *
 * The single source of truth for the exam. scripts/build-exam.js renders the
 * Canvas QTI package from this file and nothing else, so the questions are
 * edited here and never in the generated XML.
 *
 * ── Why version 3 exists ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Version 2 was generated by a language model, read well, and was rated highly
 * by a five-persona review. All forty of its answer keys were historically
 * correct. It was still unusable, and the reasons are worth keeping written
 * down because none of them is visible to a reader:
 *
 *   1. The key was strictly the longest option in 38 of 40 items. A student who
 *      knew no history and always picked the longest option scored 95%.
 *   2. Absolutist words appeared in 22% of distractors and 2% of keys, so
 *      "cross off whatever sounds too strong" was a second free signal.
 *   3. Three items told students to read a Southern Song map. The image was
 *      instructional-maps/foundations-5.svg, which is the six AP regions map.
 *      It shows no Yangtze, no Lin'an and no Quanzhou. The alt text describing
 *      a Song map had been written to match a plausible filename.
 *   4. Eight items asked which statement "best demonstrates contextualization",
 *      or causation, or comparison. That tests whether a student recognizes a
 *      vocabulary word, and combined with cue 1 those eight were free.
 *   5. Christianity never appeared. Islam, Hinduism and Daoism appeared only as
 *      wrong answers. The Mediterranean sea lanes were never a correct answer.
 *      Every one of those sits inside a stated Foundations learning target.
 *   6. Twelve items hotlinked images, two of them to commons.wikimedia.org,
 *      which returned HTTP 429 under test. An exam cannot depend on a third
 *      party being reachable during the exam.
 *   7. One item referred to "the interpretation in Question 25", which points
 *      at nothing as soon as Canvas shuffles the order.
 *
 * scripts/check-exam-cues.js is the gate on 1, 2, 5, 6 and 7. It runs in the
 * offline suite, and it fails on a V2-shaped exam. What it cannot check is
 * whether a key is historically right, which stays a teacher's judgement.
 *
 * ── How this version is built ────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Every item belongs to a stimulus set, because that is the shape of the real
 * AP World multiple-choice section: a source, then questions about it. V2 left
 * 28 of 40 items with no stimulus at all and carried no text sources whatever.
 *
 * Every image is a repository file that students already load daily from the
 * course site, and every one has been checked against what it actually depicts.
 * The captions in the Foundations data files are the reference for that.
 *
 * The passages are written for this examination and are labelled as such in
 * `provenance`. Nothing here is attributed to a historian who did not write it,
 * and no quotation, statistic or date has been invented. Where a real excerpt
 * would teach better, swap one in and keep the label honest.
 *
 * The AP skills are assessed by requiring the skill on real content rather than
 * by asking students to identify its name. `skill` records which one an item
 * leans on so the teacher can read coverage, and it is never the answer.
 */

