      provenance: WRITTEN_FOR_EXAM,
      passage:
        '<p>Four networks carried Afro-Eurasian trade before c. 1200. The Silk Roads crossed deserts and mountains by camel and pack animal. The trans-Saharan caravans crossed sand that kills horses, and became practical at scale once the camel saddle let traders load heavy freight. The Indian Ocean lanes ran on the monsoon and on ships such as the dhow, rigged with a lateen sail that could make ground across the wind. The Mediterranean lanes tied Europe, North Africa and Southwest Asia into a single basin.</p>' +
        '<p>Along all four moved goods. Along all four also moved crops, techniques, beliefs, people and disease.</p>',
